                COMPILING THE CODE & MACHINE SPECIFIC PARAMETERS

        The 'makefile' to be used with the UNIX 'make' utility is structured to be
run in a directory with two subdirectories, 'SOURCE' and 'OBJECTS'. The  
subdirectory 'SOURCE' contains each subroutine as an independent file. The
'makefile' puts the object files in subdirectory 'OBJECTS'. Local variables
no longer need to be saved when compiling by using the machine dependent save
option. The only compile options used are '-O' to optimize and '-c' to
suppress linking and produce an object file from each source file.

        The subroutine loop.f does not have the optimization flag in the makefile.
The solar single scattering calculation is slightly off when the Hp optimizes loop.f,
but the Sun and SGI optimizers are fine. If you choose to optimize loop.f ,check
the solar single scattering numbers in tape6 for case1 against the numbers in case1.tp6.
        
        On the SGI subroutine driver.f the '-O' flag gives the warning:

uopt: Warning: driver_: this procedure not optimized because it
      exceeds size threshold; to optimize this procedure, use -Olimit option
      with value >= 1209.

This is not a problem in that the code compiles and runs; driver.f is simply not
optimized. driver.f can be optimized by changing the '-O' flag to '-Olimit 1209'.

        There are a couple of machine dependent parameters in the code.  
The file 'd1mach.f' contains double precision machine constants used
in the DISORT multiple scattering section. The comments in 'd1mach.f'
explain what appropriate changes need to be made.

        The record length, 'RECL', used for reading and writing the binary band   
model file 'MOLBMP96.BIN' is machine dependent and  needs to be put into 
the open statement for 'MOLBMP96.BIN' in 'Source.dir/SOURCE/bmdata.f'. The
correct number for the record length size, 'RECL', is determined when the  
binary file 'MOLBMP96.BIN' is written by 'molbmp.f' in subdirectory 'DATA'. RECL is 60
for the Hp and Sun and is 15 for the SGI. It is important to make the binary band model
tape before compiling the main MODTRAN 3.5 executable.

        The file 'PARAM.LST' contains the list of parameters used
to change the number of layers. This file is incorporated into the
MODTRAN 3.5 code with "include" statements.


